(*
# Render: Raster3D
The triangle fillers. **This file is machine-generated - do not edit by hand.**

| | |
|---|---|
| generator | `dev/spike/itemsprite/j2lape.py` |
| source | `runelite-cache/cache/src/main/java/net/runelite/cache/item/Graphics3D.java` |
| methods | `gouraudRaster` (`:731-849`, hi-res half only), `gouraudTriangle` (`:114-730`), `method2842` (`:1410-1524`), `rasterFlat` (`:850-1407`), `rasterTextureAffine` / `method2791` / `method2794` |

Regenerate, and verify that what is committed is what the generator emits:

```bash
python3 dev/spike/itemsprite/j2lape.py \
  runelite-cache/cache/src/main/java/net/runelite/cache/item/Graphics3D.java \
  > /tmp/gen_graphics3d.simba
diff /tmp/gen_graphics3d.simba \
  <(sed -n '/^\/\/ GENERATED by/,$p' WaspLib/utils/cache/dump/render/raster3d.simba)
```

Everything from the `// GENERATED by` banner down is that output verbatim. Only
this docstring and the two include lines are hand-written.

## Why a transpiler rather than a hand port
`gouraudTriangle` is 617 Java lines of three vertex-ordering cases x four
clipping sub-cases x two slope cases - 24 near-identical `gouraudRaster` call
sites differing only in argument order and which coordinate is clamped.
`rasterFlat` is the same matrix again, 557 lines, with `method2842` as the span
filler. It is mechanical, repetitive, and exactly the shape of code a human
transcribes with a silent error in one of the 48 branches.

The load-bearing thing the generator does is the shift: Java's `>>` is
arithmetic, Lape's `shr` is logical, and this code shifts negative fixed-point
values constantly. Every `>>` becomes a {ref}`JSar` call, mechanically, with no
judgement call about which operands "can" go negative.

The second thing it does is Java's **evaluation order** for `a[i++] = expr`.
JLS 15.26.1 evaluates the index - and therefore the `++` - before the
right-hand side, so `a[i++] = a[i]` means `a[old] = a[old + 1]`. The generator
emits the increment first and stores at `i - 1`. Written the other way round,
`method2842`'s `rasterAlpha = 254` branch degenerates into `a[i] := a[i]`, a
loop that writes nothing - and that branch is live: 584 items of cache 2625
carry a face with alpha `-2`, which {ref}`LightModel` reroutes to render type 3
and hence to `rasterFlat`.

## Transpiling this file does not contradict "OS1 is the authority"
`runelite-cache` has proven defects in its palette builder, in `computeNormals`
and in its item loader - **all outside the triangle fillers**, which are
faithful scanline walkers. The boundary is deliberate: the units around this
one are hand-ported against `LostCityRS/OS1`, and only the fillers come from
the fork. See the plan's 4.

## What is deliberately absent
- the `rasterGouraudLowRes = true` half of `gouraudRaster` - icons force
  `lowDetail = false` (render spec 1), so it is unreachable;
- the low-detail branch of `method2791`: the item texture provider is fixed at
  128×128, so `TextureLowDetail` is always false. It remains generated but is
  unreachable, exactly as it is in the client item path.

`method2842` is **not** split that way: unlike `gouraudRaster` it is 4x-unrolled
unconditionally, with a `(x1 - x0) and 3` remainder loop, so it writes exactly
`x1 - x0` pixels whether or not low detail is on. There is no half to drop.
*)
